Z||000000||K2m4||11-13-2001||11:40 AM||||To all of those that are really smart and can explain this to the Shadetree mechanic...<BR>ok on the 2.8l engines...How does the EGR and PCV interact or control oil pressure if it does at all. My question stems from a problem that i had with erratic idling with a whacky oil pressure guage (it was all over the map). I had no codes but read the book and the archives and decided that problem was either at the EGR or the PCV. So I started with the cheaper part and it fixed my problem both of them especially the annoying oil pressure guage that jumped everywhere. How does this particular part effect the PCV or vice versa?<P>Kerm <BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4 ||||reg||1
Z||000001||TylerB11||11-13-2001||11:54 AM||||PCV- Positive Crankcase ventilation. Its for lettting air out of the crankcase. If it becomes busted and won't open It causes air pressure in the case, messing with the oil delivery system. I think. Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierocritter||11-13-2001||11:54 AM||||A PCV will effect your oil pressure if it gets clogged because your crankcase cannot breathe due to the expanding gasses inside the engine, thus making your oil pressure Flip out. Example: an 87 Dodge Ram had a bad pcv. Symptoms were the oil had condensation in it. again the engine could not get rid of it because of the clogged pcv. When you shut your engine off you get condensation due to cooling, outside humidity, etc. On the dodge ram it had actually started to push the valve cover seals out due to the pressure inside. I hope that helped!||||reg||1
Z||000003||watts||11-13-2001||12:19 PM||||What was said above...  also...<P>EGR (Exhaust Gas Recirculation) has nothing whatsoever to do with oil pressure.  It's an emmissions device used to pull some of the unburnt mixture coming out of your exhaust back into the intake stream to be reburnt, and it also cools the mixture somewhat to reduce knock/ping.<P>You can run without it (but why bother), but you might have to back off on your timing if you're getting any pinging.<P>As for your actual question - if your gauge is all over the place - check your grounds first, then try swapping out the sender unit (located right above your oil filter on a V6).<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierocritter||11-13-2001||12:24 PM||||totaly agree with watts||||reg||1
Z||000005||K2m4||11-13-2001||12:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B>What was said above...  also...<P>As for your actual question - if your gauge is all over the place - check your grounds first, then try swapping out the sender unit (located right above your oil filter on a V6).<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I replaced the PVC and the idle smoothed out and the oil pressure gauge steadied out... <P>Thanks for your replies. I am a very 'read the book and fix it' kind of guy not really understanding whats doing what but knowing enough to see whats gone wrong if that made any sense! My GT has so many anomolies that it seems like im forever confused..even through all this i manage to keep her running like a champ!<P><BR>kerm <BR>86 GT<BR>86 2m4<BR>||||reg||1
